Guard the omp backend's platform split with _WIN32, not WIN32.

omp_queue.cpp already carries correct dual-platform code (unistd.h
sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE) on POSIX, Windows.h GetSystemInfo on Windows) but
selects between them with plain WIN32 — which is NOT a compiler builtin.
It only exists when the build system defines it (CMake's Windows-MSVC
default flags inject /DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS); any build that sets
CMAKE_C(XX)_FLAGS explicitly loses it and the POSIX branch is compiled
on Windows:

    src/runtime/omp/omp_queue.cpp(44,10):
        fatal error: 'unistd.h' file not found

_WIN32 is predefined by every Windows compiler (MSVC, clang-cl, clang,
MinGW GCC) independent of build flags, making the selection portable
across flag regimes.

This is a BACKPORT: upstream develop already carries exactly this change
(all three sites read _WIN32 there as of 2026-08-10), so the patch
retires by itself at the next release bump. Nothing to file upstream.

diff --git a/src/runtime/omp/omp_queue.cpp b/src/runtime/omp/omp_queue.cpp
--- a/src/runtime/omp/omp_queue.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/omp/omp_queue.cpp
@@ -34,13 +34,13 @@
 #include "hipSYCL/runtime/adaptivity_engine.hpp"
 #include "hipSYCL/runtime/omp/omp_code_object.hpp"
 
-#ifndef WIN32 // MSVC might not have #warning?
+#ifndef _WIN32 // MSVC might not have #warning?
 #ifndef _OPENMP
 #warning Building omp backend with JIT support, but OpenMP parallelization is not available - kernels will run sequentially! This points to an issue in the build system.
 #endif
 #endif
 
-#ifndef WIN32
+#ifndef _WIN32
 #include <unistd.h>
 #else
 #include <Windows.h>
@@ -183,7 +183,7 @@
 #ifdef HIPSYCL_WITH_SSCP_COMPILER
 
 std::size_t get_page_size() {
-#ifndef WIN32
+#ifndef _WIN32
   return static_cast<std::size_t>(sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE));
 #else
   SYSTEM_INFO si;
